Use `assert` for a condition that must already be true.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    assert(index < items.length())
    assert(index < items.length(), "index must be in bounds")
]

The message is optional. When you add one it is an ordinary `String?` value,
including a local string, runtime template snapshot or infallible string-producing call.
It is still checked when the condition is the literal `true`, but inactive
message work does not become a generated function or runtime fact.
JavaScript and HTML evaluate it only on the failure edge; HTML-Wasm currently
rejects reachable runtime message construction.

A failed assertion stops the current program path.

It is not a recoverable error and cannot be caught.

Use `Error!` for failures callers are expected to handle. Use `assert` for
broken assumptions and impossible states.
